output:
{
  "summary": "An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge. Central to Physics 201 is Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd), governed by the core relationship $p = q \\cdot d$ (C\\cdot m (Coulomb-meter) for dipole moment; N\\cdot m for torque).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}$ with units C\\cdot m (Coulomb-meter) for dipole moment; N\\cdot m for torque.",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Students frequently confuse the direction of the dipole moment vector \\vec{p} (which points from -q to +q by physics convention) with the direction of electric field lines (which point from +q to -q).",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: Think of an electric dipole in an external field like a compass needle in Earth's magnetic field: the net push is zero so it doesn't fly across the room, but the opposing forces on its two ends twist it until it aligns with the field."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd)",
      "body": "An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** Think of an electric dipole in an external field like a compass needle in Earth's magnetic field: the net push is zero so it doesn't fly across the room, but the opposing forces on its two ends twist it until it aligns with the field."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\n\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. Force on +q is F_+ = +qE and on -q is F_- = -qE, yielding zero net translational force in a uniform field.\n2. Taking torque about the center: \\tau = (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) + (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) = qdE \\sin\\theta.\n3. Recognizing p = qd, we obtain \\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E} and potential energy U = -\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{E}."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "A dipole with charges \\pm 2.0\\,\\mu\\text{C} separated by d = 4.0\\,\\text{mm} is placed at 30^\\circ to a uniform field E = 5.0 \\times 10^4\\,\\text{N/C}. Dipole moment p = (2\\times 10^{-6})(4\\times 10^{-3}) = 8.0\\times 10^{-9}\\,\\text{C}\\cdot\\text{m}. Torque \\tau = pE\\sin 30^\\circ = 2.0\\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Students frequently confuse the direction of the dipole moment vector \\vec{p} (which points from -q to +q by physics convention) with the direction of electric field lines (which point from +q to -q)."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on Electric Dipoles, Field Lines, and Torque in Uniform Electric Fields. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. Think of an electric dipole in an external field like a compass needle in Earth's magnetic field: the net push is zero so it doesn't fly across the room, but the opposing forces on its two ends twist it until it aligns with the field. Formally, An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Students frequently confuse the direction of the dipole moment vector \\vec{p} (which points from -q to +q by physics convention) with the direction of electric field lines (which point from +q to -q). Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: A dipole with charges \\pm 2.0\\,\\mu\\text{C} separated by d = 4.0\\,\\text{mm} is placed at 30^\\circ to a uniform field E = 5.0 \\times 10^4\\,\\text{N/C}. Dipole moment p = (2\\times 10^{-6})(4\\times 10^{-3}) = 8.0\\times 10^{-9}\\,\\text{C}\\cdot\\text{m}. Torque \\tau = pE\\sin 30^\\circ = 2.0\\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}."
}

