{
  "summary": "Fitts's Law models the time required to rapidly move a pointer or thumb to a target area as a logarithmic function of the ratio between the distance to the target (D) and the width of the target (W). Central to UI/UX & Product Design is Quantitative Ergonomics & Interaction Cost, governed by the core relationship $RT = a + b \\log_2(n)$ (Milliseconds (ms) or seconds (s) for movement/reaction time; bits for Index of Difficulty).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: Fitts's Law models the time required to rapidly move a pointer or thumb to a target area as a logarithmic function of the ratio between the distance to the target (D) and the width of the target (W).",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $MT = a + b \\log_2\\left(\\frac{2D}{W}\\right)$ with units Milliseconds (ms) or seconds (s) for movement/reaction time; bits for Index of Difficulty.",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Applying Hick's Law blindly by hiding all navigation items inside deep nested hamburger menus increases interaction cost (tap count and working memory load) rather than simplifying decision-making.",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: Fitts's Law is like tossing a crumpled paper into a trash can: a wide bin right next to your desk is effortless to hit, while a tiny cup across the room requires slow, careful aim."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Quantitative Ergonomics & Interaction Cost",
      "body": "Fitts's Law models the time required to rapidly move a pointer or thumb to a target area as a logarithmic function of the ratio between the distance to the target (D) and the width of the target (W).\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** Fitts's Law is like tossing a crumpled paper into a trash can: a wide bin right next to your desk is effortless to hit, while a tiny cup across the room requires slow, careful aim."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\nMT = a + b \\log_2\\left(\\frac{2D}{W}\\right)\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. Define Index of Difficulty ID = \\log_2(2D/W) measured in bits.\n2. Movement Time MT scales linearly with ID: MT = a + b \\cdot ID.\n3. On mobile touchscreens, placing primary actions in the natural thumb zone minimizes D while enforcing a minimum 44x44px hit target maximizes W."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "Moving a mobile checkout button from the top-right corner (D = 480px, W = 32px, ID = 4.91 bits) to a full-width sticky bottom bar (D = 120px, W = 320px, ID = 0.81 bits) reduces movement difficulty by over 80%."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Applying Hick's Law blindly by hiding all navigation items inside deep nested hamburger menus increases interaction cost (tap count and working memory load) rather than simplifying decision-making."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on Fitts's Law, Hick's Law, and Cognitive Load in Responsive Mobile Interfaces. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. Fitts's Law is like tossing a crumpled paper into a trash can: a wide bin right next to your desk is effortless to hit, while a tiny cup across the room requires slow, careful aim. Formally, Fitts's Law models the time required to rapidly move a pointer or thumb to a target area as a logarithmic function of the ratio between the distance to the target (D) and the width of the target (W). When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Applying Hick's Law blindly by hiding all navigation items inside deep nested hamburger menus increases interaction cost (tap count and working memory load) rather than simplifying decision-making. Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: Moving a mobile checkout button from the top-right corner (D = 480px, W = 32px, ID = 4.91 bits) to a full-width sticky bottom bar (D = 120px, W = 320px, ID = 0.81 bits) reduces movement difficulty by over 80%."
}

