{
  "summary": "Dijkstra's algorithm computes the single-source shortest path from a starting vertex s to all other vertices in a directed or undirected graph with non-negative edge weights. Central to Computer Science — Algorithms is Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs, governed by the core relationship $d[v] = \\min(d[v], d[u] + w(u, v))$ (Asymptotic time and space complexity in vertices V and edges E).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: Dijkstra's algorithm computes the single-source shortest path from a starting vertex s to all other vertices in a directed or undirected graph with non-negative edge weights.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $O((V + E) \\log V)$ with units Asymptotic time and space complexity in vertices V and edges E.",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Using Dijkstra's algorithm on graphs with negative edge weights produces incorrect shortest paths because once a vertex is popped from the min-heap, Dijkstra assumes its distance can never be improved.",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: Imagine water spreading at a steady speed through a network of pipes of different lengths: the moment water reaches a junction for the first time, that path is guaranteed to be the fastest route."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs",
      "body": "Dijkstra's algorithm computes the single-source shortest path from a starting vertex s to all other vertices in a directed or undirected graph with non-negative edge weights.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** Imagine water spreading at a steady speed through a network of pipes of different lengths: the moment water reaches a junction for the first time, that path is guaranteed to be the fastest route."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\nO((V + E) \\log V)\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. Initialize dist[s] = 0 and dist[v] = \\infty for all v \\neq s; push (0, s) into a min-heap.\n2. Extract vertex u with minimum dist[u]; for each outgoing edge (u, v) with weight w \\ge 0, if dist[u] + w < dist[v], update dist[v] = dist[u] + w and push to heap.\n3. With a binary min-heap, V extractions take O(V \\log V) and E relaxations take O(E \\log V)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "Given edges A->B (4), A->C (1), C->B (2): start at A (dist=0). Relax B=4, C=1. Pop C (dist=1), relax C->B: 1 + 2 = 3 < 4, so update dist[B] = 3."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Using Dijkstra's algorithm on graphs with negative edge weights produces incorrect shortest paths because once a vertex is popped from the min-heap, Dijkstra assumes its distance can never be improved."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm, Priority Queues, and Relaxation. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. Imagine water spreading at a steady speed through a network of pipes of different lengths: the moment water reaches a junction for the first time, that path is guaranteed to be the fastest route. Formally, Dijkstra's algorithm computes the single-source shortest path from a starting vertex s to all other vertices in a directed or undirected graph with non-negative edge weights. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Using Dijkstra's algorithm on graphs with negative edge weights produces incorrect shortest paths because once a vertex is popped from the min-heap, Dijkstra assumes its distance can never be improved. Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: Given edges A->B (4), A->C (1), C->B (2): start at A (dist=0). Relax B=4, C=1. Pop C (dist=1), relax C->B: 1 + 2 = 3 < 4, so update dist[B] = 3."
}

