{
  "summary": "MVCC allows concurrent readers and writers to operate without blocking each other by maintaining multiple timestamped versions of each tuple. Central to Database Systems is Multi-Version Concurrency Control & Snapshot Isolation, governed by the core relationship $\\text{ReadView}(T_i) = \\{v \\mid \\text{tx}_{\\text{min}} \\le v < \\text{tx}_{\\text{max}}\\}$ (Transactions per second (TPS) and Log Sequence Numbers (LSN)).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: MVCC allows concurrent readers and writers to operate without blocking each other by maintaining multiple timestamped versions of each tuple.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $T_{\\text{commit}} \\implies \\text{LSN}_{\\text{disk}} \\ge \\text{LSN}_{\\text{tx}}$ with units Transactions per second (TPS) and Log Sequence Numbers (LSN).",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Assuming Snapshot Isolation prevents all serialization anomalies—write skew can still occur when two concurrent transactions read overlapping data and write to disjoint rows.",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: MVCC is like Google Docs version history: while an editor is typing a new draft, readers can still view the last published snapshot without seeing half-typed sentences."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Multi-Version Concurrency Control & Snapshot Isolation",
      "body": "MVCC allows concurrent readers and writers to operate without blocking each other by maintaining multiple timestamped versions of each tuple.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** MVCC is like Google Docs version history: while an editor is typing a new draft, readers can still view the last published snapshot without seeing half-typed sentences."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\nT_{\\text{commit}} \\implies \\text{LSN}_{\\text{disk}} \\ge \\text{LSN}_{\\text{tx}}\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. When transaction T updates a row, the database writes a new version tagged with tx_id = T rather than overwriting in place.\n2. Concurrent readers inspect the latest version committed prior to their snapshot timestamp.\n3. Before acknowledging COMMIT, the WAL protocol flushes the redo log record to non-volatile storage."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "User A starts a report query at t=10. At t=12, User B updates a balance from $100 to $250 and commits. User A's query continues reading the t=10 version ($100), guaranteeing repeatable reads."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Assuming Snapshot Isolation prevents all serialization anomalies—write skew can still occur when two concurrent transactions read overlapping data and write to disjoint rows."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on ACID Transactions, Write-Ahead Logging (WAL), and MVCC Concurrency Control. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. MVCC is like Google Docs version history: while an editor is typing a new draft, readers can still view the last published snapshot without seeing half-typed sentences. Formally, MVCC allows concurrent readers and writers to operate without blocking each other by maintaining multiple timestamped versions of each tuple. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Assuming Snapshot Isolation prevents all serialization anomalies—write skew can still occur when two concurrent transactions read overlapping data and write to disjoint rows. Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: User A starts a report query at t=10. At t=12, User B updates a balance from $100 to $250 and commits. User A's query continues reading the t=10 version ($100), guaranteeing repeatable reads."
}

