{
  "summary": "The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve. Central to Macroeconomics is Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium, governed by the core relationship $k_G = \\frac{1}{1 - c(1 - t)}$ (Real output Y in currency units; real interest rate r in percentage points).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $Y = C(Y - T) + I(r) + G, \\quad \\frac{M}{P} = L(r, Y)$ with units Real output Y in currency units; real interest rate r in percentage points.",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier.",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: The IS and LM curves are like two blades of a pair of scissors: neither goods spending nor money supply alone sets national income & interest rates—both blades cut together."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium",
      "body": "The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** The IS and LM curves are like two blades of a pair of scissors: neither goods spending nor money supply alone sets national income & interest rates—both blades cut together."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\nY = C(Y - T) + I(r) + G, \\quad \\frac{M}{P} = L(r, Y)\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. Goods market equilibrium Y = C + I(r) + G yields a downward-sloping IS curve because higher interest rates r reduce investment I(r).\n2. Money market equilibrium M/P = L(r, Y) yields an upward-sloping LM curve because higher income Y raises transaction demand for money, requiring higher r.\n3. Simultaneous solution determines equilibrium (Y^*, r^*)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "With marginal propensity to consume c = 0.75 and no taxes, a $10B government spending increase shifts the IS curve right by \\Delta Y = 10B / (1 - 0.75) = $40B before interest rate crowding out."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on IS-LM Model, Fiscal Multiplier, Crowding Out, and Monetary Policy Transmission. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. The IS and LM curves are like two blades of a pair of scissors: neither goods spending nor money supply alone sets national income & interest rates—both blades cut together. Formally, The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier. Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: With marginal propensity to consume c = 0.75 and no taxes, a $10B government spending increase shifts the IS curve right by \\Delta Y = 10B / (1 - 0.75) = $40B before interest rate crowding out."
}

