{
  "summary": "In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R. Central to Electrical Engineering is Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth, governed by the core relationship $Z(j\\omega) = R + j\\left(\\omega L - \\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right)$ (Resonant frequency \\omega_0 in rad/s; Quality factor Q is dimensionless).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}, \\quad Q = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}}$ with units Resonant frequency \\omega_0 in rad/s; Quality factor Q is dimensionless.",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Assuming voltage across the inductor or capacitor cannot exceed the source voltage—at resonance, V_L and V_C equal Q \\cdot V_source, which can be many times larger than the input voltage!",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: Pushing a child on a playground swing at its natural rhythm (\\omega_0) builds up huge swings (Q \\cdot V_source) from tiny pushes because the energy sloshes smoothly between height (capacitor) and speed (inductor)."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth",
      "body": "In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** Pushing a child on a playground swing at its natural rhythm (\\omega_0) builds up huge swings (Q \\cdot V_source) from tiny pushes because the energy sloshes smoothly between height (capacitor) and speed (inductor)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\n\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}, \\quad Q = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}}\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. Total complex impedance is Z(j\\omega) = R + j(\\omega L - 1/(\\omega C)).\n2. Set imaginary part to zero: \\omega_0 L = 1/(\\omega_0 C) \\implies \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{LC}.\n3. Half-power (-3 dB) bandwidth is \\Delta\\omega = R/L = \\omega_0 / Q."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "For R = 10\\,\\Omega, L = 1\\,\\text{mH}, C = 1\\,\\mu\\text{F}: \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{10^{-9}} = 31,623\\,\\text{rad/s} (5.03\\,\\text{kHz}), and Q = (1/10)\\sqrt{10^{-3}/10^{-6}} = 3.16."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Assuming voltage across the inductor or capacitor cannot exceed the source voltage—at resonance, V_L and V_C equal Q \\cdot V_source, which can be many times larger than the input voltage!"
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on RLC Resonance, Transfer Functions, Bode Plots, and Quality Factor (Q). Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. Pushing a child on a playground swing at its natural rhythm (\\omega_0) builds up huge swings (Q \\cdot V_source) from tiny pushes because the energy sloshes smoothly between height (capacitor) and speed (inductor). Formally, In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Assuming voltage across the inductor or capacitor cannot exceed the source voltage—at resonance, V_L and V_C equal Q \\cdot V_source, which can be many times larger than the input voltage! Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: For R = 10\\,\\Omega, L = 1\\,\\text{mH}, C = 1\\,\\mu\\text{F}: \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{10^{-9}} = 31,623\\,\\text{rad/s} (5.03\\,\\text{kHz}), and Q = (1/10)\\sqrt{10^{-3}/10^{-6}} = 3.16."
}

