{
  "summary": "SN2 is a concerted, one-step bimolecular substitution resulting in complete Walden inversion of configuration, whereas SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution via a planar carbocation intermediate yielding racemization. Central to Organic Chemistry is Bimolecular Concerted Backside Attack vs Unimolecular Carbocation Intermediate, governed by the core relationship $\\Delta G^\\ddagger = \\Delta H^\\ddagger - T\\Delta S^\\ddagger$ (Reaction rate in M\\cdot s^{-1}; rate constant k in M^{-1}s^{-1} (SN2) or s^{-1} (SN1)).",
  "keyTakeaways": [
    "Core Definition: SN2 is a concerted, one-step bimolecular substitution resulting in complete Walden inversion of configuration, whereas SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution via a planar carbocation intermediate yielding racemization.",
    "Fundamental Formulation: $r_{\\text{SN2}} = k[\\text{R-LG}][\\text{Nu}^-], \\quad r_{\\text{SN1}} = k[\\text{R-LG}]$ with units Reaction rate in M\\cdot s^{-1}; rate constant k in M^{-1}s^{-1} (SN2) or s^{-1} (SN1).",
    "Exam Pitfall to Avoid: Using a polar protic solvent (like water or ethanol) when attempting an SN2 reaction cages the anionic nucleophile in hydrogen bonds, drastically slowing SN2 and favoring SN1/E1 pathways.",
    "Intuitive Mental Model: SN2 is like walking through a revolving door as someone else exits the opposite side at the exact same instant (umbrella flips inside out), while SN1 is waiting for a room to empty completely before entering from either door."
  ],
  "sections": [
    {
      "heading": "1. Formal Definition & Conceptual Foundation — Bimolecular Concerted Backside Attack vs Unimolecular Carbocation Intermediate",
      "body": "SN2 is a concerted, one-step bimolecular substitution resulting in complete Walden inversion of configuration, whereas SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution via a planar carbocation intermediate yielding racemization.\n\n**Intuitive Analogy:** SN2 is like walking through a revolving door as someone else exits the opposite side at the exact same instant (umbrella flips inside out), while SN1 is waiting for a room to empty completely before entering from either door."
    },
    {
      "heading": "2. Mathematical Formulation & Step-by-Step Derivation",
      "body": "$$\nr_{\\text{SN2}} = k[\\text{R-LG}][\\text{Nu}^-], \\quad r_{\\text{SN1}} = k[\\text{R-LG}]\n$$\n\n**Derivation Steps:**\n1. In SN2, the nucleophile attacks the \\sigma^* antibonding orbital 180^\\circ opposite the leaving group; steric hindrance on tertiary carbons blocks backside approach.\n2. In SN1, the rate-determining step is heterolytic cleavage of the C-LG bond to form a sp^2 carbocation stabilized by hyperconjugation and inductive effects (3^\\circ > 2^\\circ \\gg 1^\\circ)."
    },
    {
      "heading": "3. Complete Worked Exam Problem",
      "body": "Reacting (R)-2-bromobutane with NaCN in DMSO (polar aprotic) proceeds via SN2 to yield pure (S)-2-cyanobutane with 100% inversion of stereochemistry."
    },
    {
      "heading": "4. High-Yield Exam Traps & Boundary Conditions",
      "body": "Using a polar protic solvent (like water or ethanol) when attempting an SN2 reaction cages the anionic nucleophile in hydrogen bonds, drastically slowing SN2 and favoring SN1/E1 pathways."
    }
  ],
  "lectureScript": "Welcome everyone to today's lecture on SN1 vs SN2 Nucleophilic Substitution Mechanisms, Stereochemistry, and Solvent Effects. Let's start with the big picture before we touch a single equation. SN2 is like walking through a revolving door as someone else exits the opposite side at the exact same instant (umbrella flips inside out), while SN1 is waiting for a room to empty completely before entering from either door. Formally, SN2 is a concerted, one-step bimolecular substitution resulting in complete Walden inversion of configuration, whereas SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution via a planar carbocation intermediate yielding racemization. When you sit in your exam, remember this critical trap: Using a polar protic solvent (like water or ethanol) when attempting an SN2 reaction cages the anionic nucleophile in hydrogen bonds, drastically slowing SN2 and favoring SN1/E1 pathways. Let's walk through a concrete numerical example together: Reacting (R)-2-bromobutane with NaCN in DMSO (polar aprotic) proceeds via SN2 to yield pure (S)-2-cyanobutane with 100% inversion of stereochemistry."
}

