{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for Electric Dipoles, Field Lines, and Torque in Uniform Electric Fields (Physics 201), focusing on Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd).",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd)",
      "formula": "\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}",
      "variables": "C\\cdot m (Coulomb-meter) for dipole moment; N\\cdot m for torque",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing Electric Dipoles, Field Lines, and Torque in Uniform Electric Fields problems in Physics 201."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd)",
      "definition": "An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd)",
      "steps": "1. Force on +q is F_+ = +qE and on -q is F_- = -qE, yielding zero net translational force in a uniform field.\n2. Taking torque about the center: \\tau = (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) + (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) = qdE \\sin\\theta.\n3. Recognizing p = qd, we obtain \\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E} and potential energy U = -\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{E}.\n\nWorked Example: A dipole with charges \\pm 2.0\\,\\mu\\text{C} separated by d = 4.0\\,\\text{mm} is placed at 30^\\circ to a uniform field E = 5.0 \\times 10^4\\,\\text{N/C}. Dipole moment p = (2\\times 10^{-6})(4\\times 10^{-3}) = 8.0\\times 10^{-9}\\,\\text{C}\\cdot\\text{m}. Torque \\tau = pE\\sin 30^\\circ = 2.0\\times 10^{-4}\\,\\text{N}\\cdot\\text{m}."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Students frequently confuse the direction of the dipole moment vector \\vec{p} (which points from -q to +q by physics convention) with the direction of electric field lines (which point from +q to -q)."
  ]
}

