{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm, Priority Queues, and Relaxation (Computer Science — Algorithms), focusing on Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs",
      "formula": "O((V + E) \\log V)",
      "variables": "Asymptotic time and space complexity in vertices V and edges E",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing Dijkstra's Shortest Path Algorithm, Priority Queues, and Relaxation problems in Computer Science — Algorithms."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs",
      "definition": "Dijkstra's algorithm computes the single-source shortest path from a starting vertex s to all other vertices in a directed or undirected graph with non-negative edge weights."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Greedy Edge Relaxation on Non-Negative Weighted Graphs",
      "steps": "1. Initialize dist[s] = 0 and dist[v] = \\infty for all v \\neq s; push (0, s) into a min-heap.\n2. Extract vertex u with minimum dist[u]; for each outgoing edge (u, v) with weight w \\ge 0, if dist[u] + w < dist[v], update dist[v] = dist[u] + w and push to heap.\n3. With a binary min-heap, V extractions take O(V \\log V) and E relaxations take O(E \\log V).\n\nWorked Example: Given edges A->B (4), A->C (1), C->B (2): start at A (dist=0). Relax B=4, C=1. Pop C (dist=1), relax C->B: 1 + 2 = 3 < 4, so update dist[B] = 3."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Using Dijkstra's algorithm on graphs with negative edge weights produces incorrect shortest paths because once a vertex is popped from the min-heap, Dijkstra assumes its distance can never be improved."
  ]
}

