{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for Integration by Parts, LIATE Rule, and Tabular Integration (Calculus II), focusing on Inverse Product Rule for Integrals.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Inverse Product Rule for Integrals",
      "formula": "\\int u\\,dv = u v - \\int v\\,du",
      "variables": "Dimensionless or matched to integrand differential units",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing Integration by Parts, LIATE Rule, and Tabular Integration problems in Calculus II."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Inverse Product Rule for Integrals",
      "definition": "Integration by parts transforms the integral of a product of functions into an easier integral by reversing the differential product rule."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Inverse Product Rule for Integrals",
      "steps": "1. Start from the product rule for derivatives: \\frac{d}{dx}[u(x)v(x)] = u(x)v'(x) + v(x)u'(x).\n2. Integrate both sides with respect to x: u(x)v(x) = \\int u(x)v'(x)\\,dx + \\int v(x)u'(x)\\,dx.\n3. Rearranging terms with du = u'(x)dx and dv = v'(x)dx yields \\int u\\,dv = uv - \\int v\\,du.\n\nWorked Example: Evaluate \\int x e^x\\,dx: Choose u = x (Algebraic) and dv = e^x\\,dx (Exponential). Then du = dx and v = e^x. Result: x e^x - \\int e^x\\,dx = x e^x - e^x + C = e^x(x - 1) + C."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Choosing u = e^x and dv = x\\,dx increases the power of x to x^2/2 in the new integral, making the integral progressively harder instead of simpler."
  ]
}

