{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), QLoRA Quantization, and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning (Machine Learning & AI), focusing on Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices",
      "formula": "W' = W_0 + \\Delta W = W_0 + \\frac{\\alpha}{r} B A",
      "variables": "Trainable parameters (millions) and memory footprint (GB VRAM)",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), QLoRA Quantization, and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning problems in Machine Learning & AI."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices",
      "definition": "LoRA freezes the pre-trained model weights W_0 and injects trainable low-rank decomposition matrices A and B into transformer attention layers, reducing trainable parameters by up to 99%."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices",
      "steps": "1. A full weight matrix W_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times k} has d \\times k parameters (e.g., 4096 \\times 4096 = 16.7M).\n2. Constrain the update \\Delta W = B A where rank r \\ll \\min(d, k) (e.g., r = 16).\n3. Total trainable parameters become r(d + k) = 16(4096 + 4096) = 131,072 (<1% of full fine-tuning).\n\nWorked Example: Fine-tuning a 7B parameter LLM in 4-bit NormalFloat (NF4) QLoRA with rank r=16 and alpha=32 requires only ~5.5 GB of GPU VRAM instead of >112 GB for full 16-bit optimizer states."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Setting learning rate too high (e.g., > 5e-4) or training for too many epochs on a small dataset causes catastrophic overfitting where the adapter memorizes training phrasing."
  ]
}

