{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for Michaelis-Menten Enzyme Kinetics, Lineweaver-Burk Plots, and Competitive Inhibition (Molecular Biology), focusing on Steady-State Enzyme-Substrate Reaction Rate.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Steady-State Enzyme-Substrate Reaction Rate",
      "formula": "v_0 = \\frac{V_{\\max}[S]}{K_m + [S]}",
      "variables": "Reaction velocity v_0 in \\mu\\text{M/s}; substrate concentration [S] and K_m in \\text{mM} or \\mu\\text{M}",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing Michaelis-Menten Enzyme Kinetics, Lineweaver-Burk Plots, and Competitive Inhibition problems in Molecular Biology."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Steady-State Enzyme-Substrate Reaction Rate",
      "definition": "The Michaelis-Menten equation relates the initial reaction velocity v_0 of an enzyme-catalyzed reaction to the substrate concentration [S], maximum velocity V_max, and Michaelis constant K_m."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Steady-State Enzyme-Substrate Reaction Rate",
      "steps": "1. Assume E + S <-> ES -> E + P with steady-state d[ES]/dt = 0.\n2. Rate of formation k_1[E][S] equals rate of breakdown (k_{-1} + k_2)[ES].\n3. Substituting [E]_T = [E] + [ES] and v_0 = k_2[ES] yields v_0 = V_max[S] / (K_m + [S]).\n\nWorked Example: If an enzyme has V_max = 120 \\mu\\text{M/s} and K_m = 4 \\text{mM}, at [S] = 4 \\text{mM} the velocity is exactly V_max / 2 = 60 \\mu\\text{M/s}."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Students often think a competitive inhibitor lowers V_max; in reality, competitive inhibition increases apparent K_m while V_max remains unchanged because high substrate concentrations outcompete the inhibitor."
  ]
}

