{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for IS-LM Model, Fiscal Multiplier, Crowding Out, and Monetary Policy Transmission (Macroeconomics), focusing on Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium",
      "formula": "Y = C(Y - T) + I(r) + G, \\quad \\frac{M}{P} = L(r, Y)",
      "variables": "Real output Y in currency units; real interest rate r in percentage points",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing IS-LM Model, Fiscal Multiplier, Crowding Out, and Monetary Policy Transmission problems in Macroeconomics."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium",
      "definition": "The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium",
      "steps": "1. Goods market equilibrium Y = C + I(r) + G yields a downward-sloping IS curve because higher interest rates r reduce investment I(r).\n2. Money market equilibrium M/P = L(r, Y) yields an upward-sloping LM curve because higher income Y raises transaction demand for money, requiring higher r.\n3. Simultaneous solution determines equilibrium (Y^*, r^*).\n\nWorked Example: With marginal propensity to consume c = 0.75 and no taxes, a $10B government spending increase shifts the IS curve right by \\Delta Y = 10B / (1 - 0.75) = $40B before interest rate crowding out."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier."
  ]
}

