{
  "overview": "Rapid exam revision sheet for RLC Resonance, Transfer Functions, Bode Plots, and Quality Factor (Q) (Electrical Engineering), focusing on Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth.",
  "formulaBank": [
    {
      "name": "Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth",
      "formula": "\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}, \\quad Q = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}}",
      "variables": "Resonant frequency \\omega_0 in rad/s; Quality factor Q is dimensionless",
      "whenToUse": "Apply when analyzing RLC Resonance, Transfer Functions, Bode Plots, and Quality Factor (Q) problems in Electrical Engineering."
    }
  ],
  "keyDefinitions": [
    {
      "term": "Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth",
      "definition": "In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R."
    }
  ],
  "workedDerivations": [
    {
      "title": "Derivation & Application of Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth",
      "steps": "1. Total complex impedance is Z(j\\omega) = R + j(\\omega L - 1/(\\omega C)).\n2. Set imaginary part to zero: \\omega_0 L = 1/(\\omega_0 C) \\implies \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{LC}.\n3. Half-power (-3 dB) bandwidth is \\Delta\\omega = R/L = \\omega_0 / Q.\n\nWorked Example: For R = 10\\,\\Omega, L = 1\\,\\text{mH}, C = 1\\,\\mu\\text{F}: \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{10^{-9}} = 31,623\\,\\text{rad/s} (5.03\\,\\text{kHz}), and Q = (1/10)\\sqrt{10^{-3}/10^{-6}} = 3.16."
    }
  ],
  "examTraps": [
    "Assuming voltage across the inductor or capacitor cannot exceed the source voltage—at resonance, V_L and V_C equal Q \\cdot V_source, which can be many times larger than the input voltage!"
  ]
}

