{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd) in the context of Electric Dipoles, Field Lines, and Torque in Uniform Electric Fields.",
      "back": "An electric dipole consists of two equal and opposite point charges (+q and -q) separated by a fixed distance d. The dipole moment vector points from the negative charge to the positive charge.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: Think of an electric dipole in an external field like a compass needle in Earth's magnetic..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd) and its units.",
      "back": "$$\\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E}$$ — Units/Scale: C\\cdot m (Coulomb-meter) for dipole moment; N\\cdot m for torque.",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $p = q \\cdot d$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding Electric Dipoles, Field Lines, and Torque in Uniform Electric Fields?",
      "back": "Students frequently confuse the direction of the dipole moment vector \\vec{p} (which points from -q to +q by physics convention) with the direction of electric field lines (which point from +q to -q).",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Electric Dipole Moment (p = qd).",
      "back": "1. Force on +q is F_+ = +qE and on -q is F_- = -qE, yielding zero net translational force in a uniform field.\n2. Taking torque about the center: \\tau = (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) + (d/2)(qE \\sin\\theta) = qdE \\sin\\theta.\n3. Recognizing p = qd, we obtain \\vec{\\tau} = \\vec{p} \\times \\vec{E} and potential energy U = -\\vec{p} \\cdot \\vec{E}.",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

