{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices in the context of Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), QLoRA Quantization, and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning.",
      "back": "LoRA freezes the pre-trained model weights W_0 and injects trainable low-rank decomposition matrices A and B into transformer attention layers, reducing trainable parameters by up to 99%.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: Instead of rewriting an entire 1,000-page textbook from scratch (full fine-tuning), LoRA a..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices and its units.",
      "back": "$$W' = W_0 + \\Delta W = W_0 + \\frac{\\alpha}{r} B A$$ — Units/Scale: Trainable parameters (millions) and memory footprint (GB VRAM).",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $B \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times r}, A \\in \\mathbb{R}^{r \\times k}$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding Low-Rank Adaptation (LoRA), QLoRA Quantization, and Parameter-Efficient Fine-Tuning?",
      "back": "Setting learning rate too high (e.g., > 5e-4) or training for too many epochs on a small dataset causes catastrophic overfitting where the adapter memorizes training phrasing.",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Rank-Decomposed Weight Update Matrices.",
      "back": "1. A full weight matrix W_0 \\in \\mathbb{R}^{d \\times k} has d \\times k parameters (e.g., 4096 \\times 4096 = 16.7M).\n2. Constrain the update \\Delta W = B A where rank r \\ll \\min(d, k) (e.g., r = 16).\n3. Total trainable parameters become r(d + k) = 16(4096 + 4096) = 131,072 (<1% of full fine-tuning).",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

