{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium in the context of IS-LM Model, Fiscal Multiplier, Crowding Out, and Monetary Policy Transmission.",
      "back": "The IS-LM framework models short-run macroeconomic equilibrium by intersecting the Investment-Saving (IS) curve in the goods market with the Liquidity preference-Money supply (LM) curve.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: The IS and LM curves are like two blades of a pair of scissors: neither goods spending nor..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium and its units.",
      "back": "$$Y = C(Y - T) + I(r) + G, \\quad \\frac{M}{P} = L(r, Y)$$ — Units/Scale: Real output Y in currency units; real interest rate r in percentage points.",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $k_G = \\frac{1}{1 - c(1 - t)}$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding IS-LM Model, Fiscal Multiplier, Crowding Out, and Monetary Policy Transmission?",
      "back": "Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier.",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium.",
      "back": "1. Goods market equilibrium Y = C + I(r) + G yields a downward-sloping IS curve because higher interest rates r reduce investment I(r).\n2. Money market equilibrium M/P = L(r, Y) yields an upward-sloping LM curve because higher income Y raises transaction demand for money, requiring higher r.\n3. Simultaneous solution determines equilibrium (Y^*, r^*).",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

