      "back": "Forgetting that expansionary fiscal policy raises money demand and interest rates along an upward-sloping LM curve, which partially crowds out private investment so net \\Delta Y is smaller than the simple Keynesian multiplier.",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Goods & Money Market General Equilibrium.",
      "back": "1. Goods market equilibrium Y = C + I(r) + G yields a downward-sloping IS curve because higher interest rates r reduce investment I(r).\n2. Money market equilibrium M/P = L(r, Y) yields an upward-sloping LM curve because higher income Y raises transaction demand for money, requiring higher r.\n3. Simultaneous solution determines equilibrium (Y^*, r^*).",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Hardware-Assisted Virtual-to-Physical Address Translation in the context of Virtual Memory, Page Tables, TLB Reach, and Page Fault Handling.",
      "back": "Virtual memory abstracts physical RAM into a contiguous private address space for each process using hardware page tables and a Translation Lookaside Buffer (TLB) cache.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: The TLB is like speed-dial on your phone: if a contact is on speed-dial (TLB hit), you cal..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Hardware-Assisted Virtual-to-Physical Address Translation and its units.",
      "back": "$$\\text{EAT} = \\alpha(\\epsilon + t_m) + (1 - \\alpha)(\\epsilon + 2t_m)$$ — Units/Scale: Nanoseconds (ns) for Effective Access Time (EAT); Hit Ratio \\alpha in [0, 1].",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $\\text{VPN} = \\text{VA} \\gg \\log_2(\\text{PageSize})$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding Virtual Memory, Page Tables, TLB Reach, and Page Fault Handling?",
      "back": "Confusing a TLB miss (which merely requires walking the page table in RAM, costing ~100 ns) with a hard page fault (which requires fetching a page from SSD/disk, costing millions of nanoseconds).",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Hardware-Assisted Virtual-to-Physical Address Translation.",
      "back": "1. Split virtual address into Virtual Page Number (VPN) and page offset.\n2. On a TLB hit (probability \\alpha), physical frame number is retrieved in \\epsilon ns, followed by 1 memory access (t_m).\n3. On a TLB miss (1 - \\alpha) in a single-level page table, hardware walks memory (+t_m) before accessing the data (+t_m).",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

