{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth in the context of RLC Resonance, Transfer Functions, Bode Plots, and Quality Factor (Q).",
      "back": "In a series RLC circuit, resonance occurs at the frequency \\omega_0 where inductive reactance \\omega L exactly cancels capacitive reactance 1/(\\omega C), leaving purely resistive impedance Z = R.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: Pushing a child on a playground swing at its natural rhythm (\\omega_0) builds up huge swin..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth and its units.",
      "back": "$$\\omega_0 = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{LC}}, \\quad Q = \\frac{\\omega_0 L}{R} = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{\\frac{L}{C}}$$ — Units/Scale: Resonant frequency \\omega_0 in rad/s; Quality factor Q is dimensionless.",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $Z(j\\omega) = R + j\\left(\\omega L - \\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right)$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding RLC Resonance, Transfer Functions, Bode Plots, and Quality Factor (Q)?",
      "back": "Assuming voltage across the inductor or capacitor cannot exceed the source voltage—at resonance, V_L and V_C equal Q \\cdot V_source, which can be many times larger than the input voltage!",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Frequency-Domain Impedance & Resonant Bandwidth.",
      "back": "1. Total complex impedance is Z(j\\omega) = R + j(\\omega L - 1/(\\omega C)).\n2. Set imaginary part to zero: \\omega_0 L = 1/(\\omega_0 C) \\implies \\omega_0 = 1/\\sqrt{LC}.\n3. Half-power (-3 dB) bandwidth is \\Delta\\omega = R/L = \\omega_0 / Q.",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

