{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Bimolecular Concerted Backside Attack vs Unimolecular Carbocation Intermediate in the context of SN1 vs SN2 Nucleophilic Substitution Mechanisms, Stereochemistry, and Solvent Effects.",
      "back": "SN2 is a concerted, one-step bimolecular substitution resulting in complete Walden inversion of configuration, whereas SN1 is a two-step unimolecular substitution via a planar carbocation intermediate yielding racemization.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: SN2 is like walking through a revolving door as someone else exits the opposite side at th..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Bimolecular Concerted Backside Attack vs Unimolecular Carbocation Intermediate and its units.",
      "back": "$$r_{\\text{SN2}} = k[\\text{R-LG}][\\text{Nu}^-], \\quad r_{\\text{SN1}} = k[\\text{R-LG}]$$ — Units/Scale: Reaction rate in M\\cdot s^{-1}; rate constant k in M^{-1}s^{-1} (SN2) or s^{-1} (SN1).",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $\\Delta G^\\ddagger = \\Delta H^\\ddagger - T\\Delta S^\\ddagger$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding SN1 vs SN2 Nucleophilic Substitution Mechanisms, Stereochemistry, and Solvent Effects?",
      "back": "Using a polar protic solvent (like water or ethanol) when attempting an SN2 reaction cages the anionic nucleophile in hydrogen bonds, drastically slowing SN2 and favoring SN1/E1 pathways.",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Bimolecular Concerted Backside Attack vs Unimolecular Carbocation Intermediate.",
      "back": "1. In SN2, the nucleophile attacks the \\sigma^* antibonding orbital 180^\\circ opposite the leaving group; steric hindrance on tertiary carbons blocks backside approach.\n2. In SN1, the rate-determining step is heterolytic cleavage of the C-LG bond to form a sp^2 carbocation stabilized by hyperconjugation and inductive effects (3^\\circ > 2^\\circ \\gg 1^\\circ).",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

