{
  "cards": [
    {
      "front": "Define Distributed Eventual Consistency without Two-Phase Commit (2PC) in the context of Event-Driven Microservices, Outbox Pattern, Idempotency Keys, and Saga Orchestration.",
      "back": "The Transactional Outbox Pattern guarantees atomic database state updates and message broker publication by writing outgoing domain events into a local database outbox table within the same ACID transaction.",
      "hint": "Think about the analogy: The Outbox Pattern is like dropping a stamped letter into your office's outgoing mail tray..."
    },
    {
      "front": "State the primary governing equation/complexity for Distributed Eventual Consistency without Two-Phase Commit (2PC) and its units.",
      "back": "$$P(\\text{at-least-once}) + \\text{Idempotent}(f(x)) \\implies \\text{Effectively-Once}$$ — Units/Scale: End-to-end p99 latency (ms) and message throughput (events/sec).",
      "hint": "Inline relationship: $f(f(x)) = f(x)$"
    },
    {
      "front": "What is the #1 exam misconception or pitfall regarding Event-Driven Microservices, Outbox Pattern, Idempotency Keys, and Saga Orchestration?",
      "back": "Publishing an event to Kafka/RabbitMQ inside a database transaction before COMMIT can cause downstream services to process phantom events if the local database transaction subsequently rolls back.",
      "hint": "Carefully check conventions and boundary assumptions."
    },
    {
      "front": "Walk through the key derivation steps for Distributed Eventual Consistency without Two-Phase Commit (2PC).",
      "back": "1. Dual-writing directly to a database and Kafka risks partial failure if the process crashes between the two network calls.\n2. Inserting the business row and the outbox event row in one local SQL transaction guarantees atomicity.\n3. An asynchronous relay polls or tails the transaction log (CDC) and publishes to the broker with at-least-once delivery; consumers enforce idempotency via unique event_id keys.",
      "hint": "Break it down into 3 logical steps."
    }
  ]
}

